ficulties with verbal and nonverbal communication, changes in routine, social interactions, emotional regulation, and sensory processing. Some individuals may avoid eye contact, display repetitive movements, or become overwhelmed by noise, lights, or touch. Many ASD patients experience co-occurring conditions such as anxiety, ADHD, epilepsy, sleep disorders, or gastrointestinal issues.\n\n--- chunk 4 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: What are some common characteristics and challenges experienced by individuals with Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD)?\nA: Individuals with ASD may experience difficulties with verbal and nonverbal communication, changes in routine, social interactions, emotional regulation, and sensory processing. They may also exhibit repetitive movements, avoid eye contact, or become overwhelmed by noise, lights, or touch. Many ASD patients experience co-occurring conditions such as anxiety, ADHD, epilepsy, sleep disorders, or gastrointestinal issues.\n\n--- chunk 5 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: How can employers create a supportive environment for employees with Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD)?\nA: Employers can create a supportive environment for employees with ASD by providing accommodations such as quiet workspaces or flexible scheduling, promoting inclusivity and understanding of ASD in the workplace.\n\n--- chunk 6 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: How can healthcare providers improve the healthcare experiences and outcomes for individuals with ASD?\nA: Healthcare providers should use patient-centered approaches and consider sensory needs during examinations or procedures. Understanding communication differences, sensory sensitivities, and behavioral triggers can significantly improve healthcare experiences and outcomes for individuals on the autism spectrum.\n\n--- chunk 7 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: How can clinicians create a supportive environment for patients with Autism Spectrum Disorder during examinations or procedures?\nA: Clinicians can create a supportive environment for patients with Autism Spectrum Disorder during examinations or procedures by considering their sensory needs and using patient-centered approaches. This may involve reducing unnecessary sensory stimulation, maintaining predictable routines, and informing the patient before transitions or procedures. By taking the time to understand the patient\'s unique needs and preferences, clinicians can tailor their approach to meet their individual requirements.\n\n--- chunk 8 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: How can healthcare providers improve the experience and outcomes for individuals with ASD?\nA: Understanding communication differences, sensory sensitivities, and behavioral triggers can significantly improve healthcare experiences and outcomes for individuals on the autism spectrum. Healthcare providers should use patient-centered approaches, consider sensory needs during examinations or procedures, and recognize the unique strengths and challenges of each individual.\n\nYour job: for EACH chunk, decide whether it contains information that\ndirectly helps answer the ORIGINAL USER QUESTION.\n\nA chunk is RELEVANT only if a reader could extract at least one fact from\nit that contributes to answering the question. Tangential mentions of the\ntopic, generic background, or off-topic text are NOT relevant.\n\nThen give an OVERALL verdict:\n- "PASS"    : majority of chunks are relevant AND together they could plausibly support an answer.\n- "PARTIAL" : some chunks are relevant but coverage is thin or incomplete.\n- "FAIL"    : few or no chunks are relevant; the agent should retry with a different query.\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object (no markdown, no prose, no code fences) with this exact shape:\n\n{\n  "verdict": "PASS" | "PARTIAL" | "FAIL",\n  "per_chunk": [\n    {"index": 0, "relevant": true,  "reason": "<one short sentence>"},\n    {"index": 1, "relevant": false, "reason": "<one short sentence>"}\n  ],\n  "overall_reason": "<one or two sentences>"\n}\n', '