 an individual education program (IEP) if the child is three years of age or older. A comprehensive assessment should include a complete physical exam, including assessment for dysmorphic features, a full neurologic examination with head circumference, and a Wood’s lamp examination of the skin.\n\n--- chunk 3 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: What are the common co-morbid diagnoses found in children with ASD?\nA: Children with ASD should also be screened for common co-morbid diagnoses such as anxiety, ADHD, epilepsy, sleep disorders, or gastrointestinal issues.\n\n--- chunk 4 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: What are some key strategies for supporting children with ASD in their social development?\nA: Identify possible triggers such as sensory overload, pain, anxiety, or changes in routine, and provide a supportive and understanding environment. Remain calm, avoid confrontation, and use de-escalation techniques to help children with ASD develop the skills and strategies they need to thrive.\n\n--- chunk 5 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: What is the recommended course of action for primary care clinicians when suspecting ASD in a child?\nA: Primary care clinicians are recommended to refer children to early intervention if less than 3 years of age or to the public school system for psychoeducational evaluation in order to establish an individual education program (IEP) if the child is three years of age or older.\n\n--- chunk 6 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: What should primary care clinicians do when suspecting ASD in a child?\nA: Primary care clinicians are recommended to refer children to early intervention if less than 3 years of age or to the public school system for psychoeducational evaluation in order to establish an individual education program (IEP) if the child is three years of age or older. A comprehensive assessment should include a complete physical exam, including assessment for dysmorphic features, a full neurologic examination with head circumference, and a Wood’s