ource: learned_qa) ---\nQ: Does the patient dataset reflect a notable trend in parental age among children with Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD)?\nA: Yes, the patient dataset reflects a trend of older parental age being a notable factor in the development of ASD. A study on the autism and developmental disabilities monitoring network found that children with ASD were more likely to have older parents.\n\n--- chunk 4 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: How does the patient dataset support the relationship between parental age and ASD risk?\nA: The patient dataset shows a correlation between increased parental age and the risk of ASD, consistent with previous studies. However, it is essential to note that no final causal pathway has been elucidated, and more research is required to confirm the correlation.\n\n--- chunk 5 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: What are some risk factors associated with ASD?\nA: Increased parental age and prematurity are risk factors associated with ASD. This could be due to the theory that older gametes have a higher probability of carrying mutations which could result in additional obstetrical complications.\n\n--- chunk 6 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: What are some other risk factors for Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) besides increased parental age?\nA: Other risk factors for ASD include prematurity. This could be due to the theory that older gametes have a higher probability of carrying mutations which could result in additional obstetrical complications.\n\n--- chunk 7 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: What are some known risk factors for ASD?\nA: Other risk factors for ASD include increased parental age and prematurity. This could be due to the theory that older gametes have a higher probability of carrying mutations which could result in additional obstetrical complications.\n\n--- chunk 8 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: Does the patient dataset reflect a notable trend in parental age among children with Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD)?\nA: Yes, the patient 