A: Challenges in identifying and treating ASD in diverse populations include delayed identification, postponed therapy, and limited access to specialized care. A fairer system will require clinicians, educators, and policymakers to move beyond one-size-fits-all assumptions and recognize the diverse ways autism appears across different populations.

[5] Q: Do the disparities in ASD prevalence among different racial and ethnic groups indicate that the apparent stabilization of ASD prevalence between 2014 and 2016 was an illusion?
A:

[6] Healthcare providers should use patient-centered approaches and consider sensory needs during examinations or procedures to effectively manage ASD.

[7] Gender and racial factors can impact who is recognized early, who is overlooked, and who receives appropriate services. These disparities matter because delayed identification can postpone therapy, school accommodations, family guidance, and access to specialized care.

[8] Screening tools and clinical training should reflect a broader range of autistic presentations, especially in girls and culturally diverse populations.

[9] One of the most persistent disparities in autism is the gap between boys and girls in diagnosis and recognition. Recent CDC surveillance data reported that ASD was identified far more often among boys than girls, with prevalence among boys about 3.4 times that among girls in the 2022 surveillance sample. While some biological and developmental differences may contribute to this pattern, many researchers argue that the gap also reflects underrecognition of autism in girls rather than only true differences in occurrence.

[10] A late diagnosis may lead to years of unmet support needs, emotional exhaustion, social stress, and misunderstanding by teachers, clinicians, and family members. In adolescence and adulthood, girls and women with undiagnosed autism may present with burnout, depression, or chronic feelings of isolation because their difficulties were interpreted as personality traits rather than neurodevelopmental differences.

[11] Racial and ethnic disparities in ASD are also complex. Older research often showed that Black and Hispanic children were less likely than White children to have a documented autism diagnosis in service records and were more likely to face delays, misclassification, or incomplete evaluation pathways. Barriers such as language differences, stigma, limited specialist access, provider bias, transportation problems, and weak continuity of care contributed to these gaps.

[12] Disparities in ASD cannot be understood through gender or race alone. Gender-based underrecognition, especially among girls, and racial inequities in diagnosis and care continue to shape who receives support and who is left behind. Although surveillance data suggest some progress in identification across racial and ethnic groups, significant inequities remain in timing, pathway, quality of diagnosis, and access to services. A fairer system will require clinicians, educators, and policymakers to move beyond one-size-fits-all assumptions and recognize the diverse ways autism appears across different populations.

[13] More recent CDC surveillance data show a different pattern in prevalence estimates, with ASD prevalence in recent samples being lower among White children than among several other racial and ethnic groups, including Black, Hispanic, Asian/Pacific Islander, and American Indian/Alaska Native children. However, higher observed prevalence does not mean disparities have disappeared. Instead, it may reflect improvements in recognition in some communities while major inequities remain in the timing of diagnosis, quality of assessment, comorbid condition management, and service access.

[14] ASD occurs in all racial, ethnic, and socioeconomic groups, but its diagnosis is far from uniform across these groups. Caucasian children are consistently identified with ASD more often than black or Hispanic children. ASD is more common in males, though the true male-to-female ratio is closer to 3:1. Girls who meet criteria for ASD are at higher risk of not receiving a clinical diagnosis. Females are less likely to present with overt symptoms and more likely to mask their social deficits through camouflaging, and gender biases and stereotypes of ASD as a male disorder could also hamper diagnoses in girls. Females are less likely to be diagnosed with ASD due to gender biases and stereotypes.

[15] Insurance mandates requiring commercial plans to cover services for ASD along with improved awareness have likely contributed to the increase in ASD prevalence estimates as well as the increased diagnosis of milder cases of ASD in

