Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) is a neurodevelopmental disorder that affects individuals across all racial, ethnic, and socioeconomic groups. However, its diagnosis is far from uniform, with disparities in identification and treatment across different populations. 

For example, ASD is more common in males, with a prevalence about 3.4 times that among females [9]. Girls who meet criteria for ASD are at higher risk of not receiving a clinical diagnosis, often due to underrecognition of autism in girls and gender biases in diagnosis [9, 14]. 

Racial and ethnic disparities in ASD diagnosis also exist, with Caucasian children consistently identified with ASD more often than Black or Hispanic children [14]. These disparities may be due to various factors, including language differences, stigma, limited specialist access, provider bias, and transportation problems [11].

To effectively manage ASD, healthcare providers should use patient-centered approaches and consider sensory needs during examinations or procedures [6]. It is also essential to recognize the diverse ways autism appears across different populations and move beyond one-size-fits-all assumptions [4].
