2026-07-15 14:02:35 | DEBUG    | openai._base_client | [-] | Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'headers': {'X-Stainless-Raw-Response': 'true'}, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-38a8b6c1-38b8-4a33-82b3-448543477796', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'content': 'You are a strict relevance judge for a retrieval system.\n\nORIGINAL USER QUESTION:\nBased on everything in the knowledge base, write a brief summary a non-specialist could read to understand what ASD is, how it is identified, and how it is managed.\n\nRETRIEVED CHUNKS (one per block, indexed):\n--- chunk 0 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: What are some of the disparities in ASD diagnosis and treatment?\nA: Despite increasing clinical and public attention, the experience of being identified, diagnosed, and treated is not the same for all patients. Gender and racial factors shape who is recognized early, who is overlooked, who receives appropriate services, and who must overcome greater barriers before support becomes available.\n\n--- chunk 1 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: What are the implications of racial and ethnic disparities in ASD diagnosis?\nA: Racial and ethnic disparities in ASD diagnosis can result in delays, misclassification, or incomplete evaluation pathways, highlighting the need for culturally responsive assessment frameworks and provider training to address these inequities.\n\n--- chunk 2 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: Do disparities in ASD diagnosis across racial, ethnic, and socioeconomic groups persist despite the apparent stabilization of ASD prevalence between 2014 and 2016?\nA: The continued discrepancy in ASD diagnosis across racial, ethnic, and socioeconomic groups may be due to stigma, lack of access to healthcare services, and a patient\'s primary language being one other than English.\n\n--- chunk 3 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: Do the disparities in ASD prevalence among different racial and ethnic groups indicate that the apparent stabilization of ASD prevalence between 2014 and 2016 was an illusion?\nA: Higher observed prevalence in minority groups does not mean disparities have disappeared. Instead, it may reflect improvements in recognition in some communities while major inequities remain in the timing of diagnosis, quality of assessment, comorbid condition management, and service access.\n\n--- chunk 4 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: What are the disparities in ASD diagnosis and treatment, particularly for girls and culturally diverse populations?\nA: Disparities in ASD diagnosis and treatment exist, particularly for girls and culturally diverse populations. Screening tools and clinical training should reflect a broader range of autistic presentations, especially in these groups. Providers should be trained to recognize masking, subtle social communication differences, and the ways autism may be expressed differently across communities.\n\n--- chunk 5 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: What are some of the disparities in diagnosis and treatment of autism spectrum disorder?\nA: The experience of being identified, diagnosed, and treated is not the same for all patients with ASD. Gender and racial factors shape who is recognized early, who is overlooked, and who receives appropriate services. Delayed identification can postpone therapy, school accommodations, family guidance, and access to specialized care.\n\n--- chunk 6 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: What are the challenges in identifying and treating ASD in diverse populations?\nA: Challenges in identifying and treating ASD in diverse populations include delayed identification, postponed therapy, and limited access to specialized care. A fairer system will require clinicians, educators, and policymakers to move beyond one-size-fits-all assumptions and recognize the diverse ways autism appears across different populations.\n\n--- chunk 7 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: What are some of the disparities in the experience of individuals with Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD)?\nA: The experience of being identified, diagnosed, and treated is not the same for all patients with Autism Spectrum Disorder. Gender and racial factors shape who is recognized early, who is overlooked, who receives appropriate services, and who must overcome greater barriers before support becomes available. Reducing disparities among ASD patients requires action at multiple levels, including screening tools, clinical training, and diagnostic pathways that reflect a broader range of autistic presentations.\n\n--- chunk 8 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: What are the disparities in Autism Spectrum Disorder diagnosis and treatment across different genders and racial groups?\nA: Gender and racial factors can impact who is recognized early, who is overlooked, and who receives appropriate services. These disparities matter because delayed identification can postpone therapy, school accommodations, family guidance, and access to specialized care.\n\n--- chunk 9 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: How do gender and racial factors impact Autism Spectrum Disorder diagnosis and treatment?\nA: Gender and racial factors can impact who is recognized early, who is overlooked, and who receives appropriate services. These disparities matter because delayed identification can postpone therapy, school accommodations, family guidance, and access to specialized care.\n\n--- chunk 10 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: How do gender and racial factors impact the diagnosis and treatment of Autism Spectrum Disorder?\nA: Gender and racial factors can impact who is recognized early, who is overlooked, and who receives appropriate services. These disparities matter because delayed identification can postpone therapy, school accommodations, family guidance, and access to specialized care.\n\n--- chunk 11 (source: learned_qa) ---\nQ: What are the disparities in ASD diagnosis and treatment, particularly in terms of gender and racial factors?\nA: Disparities in ASD diagnosis and treatment exist, particularly in terms of gender and racial factors. Screening tools and clinical training should reflect a broader range of autistic presentations, especially in girls and culturally diverse populations. Providers should be trained to recognize masking, subtle social communication differences, and the ways autism may be expressed differently across communities.\n\nYour job: for EACH chunk, decide whether it contains information that\ndirectly helps answer the ORIGINAL USER QUESTION.\n\nA chunk is RELEVANT only if a reader could extract at least one fact from\nit that contributes to answering the question. Tangential mentions of the\ntopic, generic background, or off-topic text are NOT relevant.\n\nThen give an OVERALL verdict:\n- "PASS"    : majority of chunks are relevant AND together they could plausibly support an answer.\n- "PARTIAL" : some chunks are relevant but coverage is thin or incomplete.\n- "FAIL"    : few or no chunks are relevant; the agent should retry with a different query.\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object (no markdown, no prose, no code fences) with this exact shape:\n\n{\n  "verdict": "PASS" | "PARTIAL" | "FAIL",\n  "per_chunk": [\n    {"index": 0, "relevant": true,  "reason": "<one short sentence>"},\n    {"index": 1, "relevant": false, "reason": "<one short sentence>"}\n  ],\n  "overall_reason": "<one or two sentences>"\n}\n', 'role': 'system'}], 'model': 'llama-3.1-8b-instruct', 'max_completion_tokens': 1024, 'stream': False, 'temperature': 0.0}}
