  "content": "Racial and ethnic disparities in ASD are also complex [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html]. Older research often showed that Black and Hispanic children were less likely than White children to have a documented autism diagnosis in service records and were more likely to face delays, misclassification, or incomplete evaluation pathways [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html]. Barriers such as language differences, stigma, limited specialist access, provider bias, transportation problems, and weak continuity of care contributed to these gaps [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html]. More recent CDC surveillance data show a different pattern in prevalence estimates, with ASD prevalence in recent samples being lower among White children than among several other racial and ethnic groups, including Black, Hispanic, Asian/Pacific Islander, and American Indian/Alaska Native children [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html]. However, higher observed prevalence does not mean disparities have disappeared [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html]. Instead, it may reflect improvements in recognition in some communities while major inequities remain in the timing of diagnosis, quality of assessment, comorbid condition management, and service access [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html]. In other words, being counted is not the same as being equitably served [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html].",
