eillance data show a different pattern in prevalence estimates, with ASD prevalence in recent samples being lower among White children than among several other racial and ethnic groups, including Black, Hispanic, Asian/Pacific Islander, and American Indian/Alaska Native children [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_rac …[truncated]\n\n--- chunk 2 (source: ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html, ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nReducing disparities among ASD patients requires action at multiple levels [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html]. Screening tools and clinical training should reflect a broader range of autistic presentations, especially in girls and culturally diverse populations [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html]. Providers should be trained to recognize masking, subtle social communication differences, and the ways autism may be expressed differently across communities [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html]. Diagnostic pathways should include interpreters, culturally responsive family communication, and patient navigation support for families facing practical barriers [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html].\n\nPROPOSED GROUPS:\nGroup 0:\n  chunk_index=0  sentence="A late diagnosis may lead to years of unmet support needs, emotional exhaustion, social stress, and misunderstanding by teachers, clinicians, and family members."\n  chunk_index=1  sentence="Barriers such as language differences, stigma, limited specialist access, provider bias, transportation problems, and weak continuity of care contributed to these gaps."\n\nGroup 1:\n  chunk_index=0  sentence="In adolescence and adulthood, girls and women with undiagnosed autism may present with burnout, depression, or chronic feelings of isolation because their difficulties were interpreted as personality traits rather than neurodevelopmental differences."\n  chunk_index=1  sentence="Instead, it may reflect improvements in recognition in some communities while major inequities remain in the timing of diagnosis, quality of assessment, comorbid condition management, and service access."\n\nGroup 2:\n  chunk_index=0  sentence="In adolescence and adulthood, girls and women with undiagnosed autism may present with burnout, depression, or chronic feelings of isolation because their difficulties were interpreted as personality traits rather than neurodevelopmental differences."\n  chunk_index=2  sentence="Providers should be trained to recognize masking, subtle social communication differences, and the ways autism may be expressed differently across communities."\n\nGroup 3:\n  chunk_index=1  sentence="More recent CDC surveillance data show a different pattern in prevalence estimates, with ASD prevalence in recent samples being lower among White children than among several other racial and ethnic groups, including Black, Hispanic, Asian/Pacific Islander, and American Indian/Alaska Native children."\n  chunk_index=2  sentence="Screening tools and clinical training should reflect a broader range of autistic presentations, especially in girls and culturally diverse populations."\n\nA group is CONFIRMED only if:\n- all sentences have the same subject\n- all sentences make the same claim\n- all sentences have the same meaning\n- removing duplicate copies would NOT lose unique information\n\nIMPORTANT:\n- Same topic is NOT enough.\n- Related ideas are NOT enough.\n- Similar wording is NOT enough.\n- Advice and explanation are NOT automatically the same fact.\n- General statements and specific instructions are NOT redundant.\n- If one sentence adds a unique detail, the group is REJECTED.\n- If the subjects are different real-world entities, the group is REJECTED.\n- If meanings conflict or one sentence negates another, the group is REJECTED.\n\nBe STRICT.\nBe CONSERVATIVE.\nIf unsure, REJECT the group.\n\nGOOD:\n"ASDs vary motor speed by cont