, ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html, ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nDisparities in ASD cannot be understood through gender or race alone [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html]. Gender-based underrecognition, especially among girls, and racial inequities in diagnosis and care continue to shape who receives support and who is left behind [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html]. Although surveillance data suggest some progress in identification across racial and ethnic groups [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html], significant inequities remain in timing, pathway, quality of diagnosis, and access to services [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html]. A fairer system will require clinicians, educators, and policymakers to move beyond one-size-fits-all assumptions [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html] and recognize the diverse ways autism appears across different populations [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html].\n\n--- chunk 2 (source: ..\\data\\pdfs\\Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf) ---\nASD occurs in all racial, ethnic, and socioeconomic groups, but its diagnosis is far from uniform across these groups. Caucasian children are consistently identified with ASD more often than black or Hispanic children (6). While the differences appear to be decreasing, the continued discrepancy may be due to stigma, lack of access to healthcare services, and a patient’s primary language being one other than English.\n\nPROPOSED GROUPS:\nGroup 0:\n  chunk_index=0  sentence="Gender and racial factors shape who is recognized early, who is overlooked, who receives appropriate services, and who must overcome greater barriers before support becomes available."\n  chunk_index=1  sentence="Gender-based underrecognition, especially among girls, and racial inequities in diagnosis and care continue to shape who receives support and who is left behind."\n\nGroup 1:\n  chunk_index=0  sentence="These disparities matter because delayed identification can postpone therapy, school accommodations, family guidance, and access to specialized care."\n  chunk_index=1  sentence="Although surveillance data suggest some progress in identification across racial and ethnic groups, significant inequities remain in timing, pathway, quality of diagnosis, and access to services."\n\nGroup 2:\n  chunk_index=0  sentence="These disparities matter because delayed identification can postpone therapy, school accommodations, family guidance, and access to specialized care."\n  chunk_index=1  sentence="A fairer system will require clinicians, educators, and policymakers to move beyond one-size-fits-all assumptions and recognize the diverse ways autism appears across different populations."\n\nGroup 3:\n  chunk_index=1  sentence="A fairer system will require clinicians, educators, and policymakers to move beyond one-size-fits-all assumptions and recognize the diverse ways autism appears across different populations."\n  chunk_index=2  sentence="While the differences appear to be decreasing, the continued discrepancy may be due to stigma, lack of access to healthcare services, and a patient’s primary language being one other than English."\n\nA group is CONFIRMED only if:\n- all sentences have the same subject\n- all sentences make the same claim\n- all sentences have the same meaning\n- removing duplicate copies would NOT lose unique information\n\nIMPORTANT:\n- Same topic is NOT enough.\n- Related ideas are NOT enough.\n- Similar wording is NOT enough.\n- Advice and explanation are NOT automatically the same fact.\n- General statements and specific instructions are NOT redundant.\n- If one sentence adds a unique detail, the group is REJECTED.\n- If the subjects are different real-world entities, the group is REJECTED.\n- If meanings conflict or one sentence negates another, the group is REJECTED.\n\nBe STRICT.\nBe CONSERVATIVE.\nIf unsure, REJECT the group.\n\nGOOD:\n"ASDs vary motor speed by controlling voltage and frequency."\n"An ASD regulates motor speed by adjusting frequency and voltage."\nVerdict: CONFIRMED\n\nBAD:\n"ASD patients may experience sensory overload."\n"Healthcare providers should reduce loud noises."\nVerdict: REJECTED\nReason: related topic, but different facts.\n\nBAD:\n"Managing ASD patients requires individualized care."\n"Reduce bright lights and loud sounds."\nVerdict: REJECTED\nReason: general guidance versus specific instruction.\n\nBAD:\n"ASDs reduce energy costs and mechanical wear."\n"ASDs reduce energy costs."\nVerdict: REJECTED\nReason: one sentence contains unique information.\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT:\nReturn ONLY a JSON array.\nOne object per proposed group, in the same order as the input groups.\n\n[\n  {\n    "group_index": 0,\n    "verdict": "CONFIRMED",\n    "reason": "Same subject and same factual claim."\n  },\n  {\n    "group_index": 1,\n    "verdict": "REJECTED",\n    "reason": "Related topic but different facts."\n  }\n]\n\nBAD OUTPUTS:\n- Python code\n- Pseudocode\n- Markdown\n- Explanations outside JSON\n- Text before or after the JSON array\n\nThe first character of your response must be \'[\'.\nYour response will be parsed directly using json.loads().\nInvalid JSON will cause failure.\n\nReturn ONLY the JSON array.\n', 'role': 'system'}], 'model': 'llama-3.1-8b-instruct', 'max_completion_tokens': 1024, 'stream': False, 'temperature': 0.0}}
