er_race_essay.html]. However, higher observed prevalence does not mean disparities have disappeared [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html]. Instead, it may reflect improvements in recognition in some communities while major inequities remain in the timing of diagnosis, quality of assessment, comorbid condition management, and service access [Source:../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html]. In other words, being counted is not the same as being equitably served [Source …[truncated]\n\nCOMPRESSED CHUNK:\nOlder research often showed that Black and Hispanic children were less likely than White children to have a documented autism diagnosis in service records and were more likely to face delays, misclassification, or incomplete evaluation pathways. Barriers such as language differences, stigma, limited specialist access, provider bias, transportation problems, and weak continuity of care contributed to these gaps. More recent CDC surveillance data show a different pattern in prevalence estimates, with ASD prevalence in recent samples being lower among White children than among several other racial and ethnic groups, including Black, Hispanic, Asian/Pacific Islander, and American Indian/Alaska Native children. However, higher observed prevalence does not mean disparities have disappeared. Instead, it may reflect improvements in recognition in some communities while major inequities remain in the timing of diagnosis, quality of assessment, comorbid condition management, and service access. In other words, being counted is not the same as being equitably served.\n\nJUDGMENT RULES:\nRULE 1 — NO FABRICATION\nEvery sentence in the COMPRESSED CHUNK must be traceable (verbatim or as a clear paraphrase)\nto the ORIGINAL CHUNK. If any sentence in the compressed output introduces new information\nnot present in the original, list it in "fabricated_claims".\n\nRULE 2 — NO RELEVANT FACTS LOST\nA sentence from the ORIGINAL CHUNK is a "lost relevant fa