rocessing. Although ASD has received increasing clinical and public attention, the experience of being identified, diagnosed, and treated is not the same for all patients. Gender and racial factors shape who is recognized early, who is overlooked, who receives appropriate services, and who must overcome greater barriers before support becomes available. These disparities matter because delayed identification can postpone therapy, school accommodations, family guidance, and access to specialized care.\n\n[7] Gender-based underrecognition, especially among girls, and racial inequities in diagnosis and care continue to shape who receives support and who is left behind. Although surveillance data suggest some progress in identification across racial and ethnic groups, significant inequities remain in timing, pathway, quality of diagnosis, and access to services. A fairer system will require clinicians, educators, and policymakers to move beyond one-size-fits-all assumptions and recognize the diverse ways autism appears across different populations.\n\n[8] ASD occurs in all racial, ethnic, and socioeconomic groups, but its diagnosis is far from uniform across these groups.\n\nA claim may appear in the draft only if:\n- it is stated in the CONTEXT, verbatim or as a direct paraphrase\n- it is not an inference, extrapolation, or generalization beyond what the CONTEXT says\n- it is not a "typical" or "common" fact about the topic that you know but the CONTEXT does not state\n\nIMPORTANT:\n- Length must track evidence, not effort. A single relevant chunk gets a short draft, not a padded one.\n- Do not pad a thin answer with plausible-sounding related facts to make it feel complete.\n- If the context only partially covers the question, say so in the draft instead of glossing over it.\n- If the context does not meaningfully address the question, say so directly instead of guessing.\n- Mentioning a topic is NOT the same as supporting a specific claim about it.\n- If the context 