2026-07-15 14:26:11 | DEBUG    | openai._base_client | [-] | Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'headers': {'X-Stainless-Raw-Response': 'true'}, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-3a66be24-679f-4e19-9270-e21a82579eda', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'content': 'You are performing sentence-level redundancy annotation.\n\nYour task:\nIdentify sentences from DIFFERENT chunks that express the SAME FACT.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nTwo sentences are redundant ONLY if they communicate essentially the SAME information.\n\nThe sentences must match on:\n- subject\n- claim\n- meaning\n\nBeing about the SAME TOPIC is NOT enough.\n\nYou are NOT writing software.\nYou are NOT generating Python.\nYou are NOT solving a coding task.\nYou are ONLY selecting matching factual statements.\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON array.\n\nCHUNKS:\n--- chunk 0 (source: ../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html, ../data/html/asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nRacial and ethnic disparities in ASD are also complex. Older research often showed that Black and Hispanic children were less likely than White children to have a documented autism diagnosis in service records and were more likely to face delays, misclassification, or incomplete evaluation pathways . Barriers such as language differences, stigma, limited specialist access, provider bias, transportation problems, and weak continuity of care contributed to these gaps . More recent CDC surveillance data show a different pattern in prevalence estimates, with ASD prevalence in recent samples being lower among White children than among several other racial and ethnic groups, including Black, Hispanic, Asian/Pacific Islander, American Indian/Alaska Native children . However, higher observed prevalence does not mean disparities have disappeared . Instead, it may reflect improvements in recognition in some communities while major inequities remain in the timing of diagnosis, quality of assessment, comorbid condition management, and service access . In other words, being counted is not the same as being equitably served .\n\n--- chunk 1 (source: ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html, ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nDisparities in ASD cannot be understood through gender or race alone . Gender-based underrecognition, especially among girls, and racial inequities in diagnosis and care continue to shape who receives support and who is left behind . Although surveillance data suggest some progress in identification across racial and ethnic groups, significant inequities remain in timing, pathway, quality of diagnosis, and access to services . A fairer system will require clinicians, educators, and policymakers to move beyond one-size-fits-all assumptions and recognize the diverse ways autism appears across different populations .\n\n--- chunk 2 (source: ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html, ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html, ..\\data\\html\\asd_disparities_gender_race_essay.html) ---\nReducing disparities among ASD patients requires action at multiple levels   . Screening tools and clinical training should reflect a broader range of autistic presentations, especially in girls and culturally diverse populations   . Providers should be trained to recognize masking, subtle social communication differences, and the ways autism may be expressed differently across communities   . Diagnostic pathways should include interpreters, culturally responsive family communication, and patient navigation support for families facing practical barriers   .\n\nWHAT COUNTS AS REDUNDANT:\n- Same fact\n- Same subject\n- Same meaning\n- Paraphrases are allowed\n\nGOOD EXAMPLE:\nChunk 0:\n"ASD affects 1 in 36 children."\n\nChunk 1:\n"Approximately 1 in 36 children have autism."\n\nThese ARE redundant because they express the same fact.\n\nANOTHER GOOD EXAMPLE:\nChunk 0:\n"ASDs regulate motor speed by changing voltage and frequency."\n\nChunk 1:\n"An ASD controls motor speed through voltage and frequency adjustment."\n\nThese ARE redundant.\n\nWHAT IS NOT REDUNDANT:\n\nNOT REDUNDANT — SAME TOPIC BUT DIFFERENT FACTS:\nChunk 0:\n"ASD patients may experience sensory overload."\n\nChunk 1:\n"Healthcare providers should reduce loud noises."\n\nThese are RELATED but NOT redundant.\n\nNOT REDUNDANT — DIFFERENT SUBJECTS:\nChunk 0:\n"Dawood\'s policy includes accidents."\n\nChunk 1:\n"Umair\'s policy includes accidents."\n\nDifferent subjects = NOT redundant.\n\nNOT REDUNDANT — GENERAL VS SPECIFIC:\nChunk 0:\n"Managing ASD patients requires individualized care."\n\nChunk 1:\n"Reduce bright lights and loud sounds."\n\nThese are NOT the same fact.\n\nIMPORTANT RULES:\n- A group MUST contain sentences from AT LEAST TWO DIFFERENT chunks.\n- Never group sentences from only one chunk.\n- One shared fact = one group.\n- Copy sentences EXACTLY as written.\n- Do not rewrite sentences.\n- Be CONSERVATIVE.\n- If unsure, DO NOT group.\n- It is BETTER to miss a redundancy than to create a false match.\n\nIf nothing is redundant, return:\n[]\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT:\n[\n  {\n    "members": [\n      {\n        "chunk_index": 0,\n        "sentence": "Exact sentence from chunk."\n      },\n      {\n        "chunk_index": 1,\n        "sentence": "Matching sentence from another chunk."\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n]\n\nBAD OUTPUTS:\n- Python\n- Pseudocode\n- Explanations\n- Markdown\n- Comments\n- Any text outside the JSON\n\nThe first character of your response must be \'[\'\n\nYour response will be parsed directly using json.loads().\nInvalid JSON will cause failure.\n\nReturn ONLY the JSON array.\n', 'role': 'system'}], 'model': 'llama-3.1-8b-instruct', 'max_completion_tokens': 2048, 'stream': False, 'temperature': 0.1}}
