Autism spectrum disorder (ASD) is a neurodevelopmental condition associated with differences in social communication, restricted interests, repetitive behaviors, and sensory processing [4]. Disparities in diagnosis and treatment exist, particularly in terms of gender and racial factors [1]. These disparities matter because delayed identification can postpone therapy, school accommodations, family guidance, and access to specialized care [4].

ASD can be difficult to identify, especially in girls and culturally diverse populations, as clinicians may carry cultural assumptions about family communication or behavior that can lead to missed diagnoses [3]. In adolescence and adulthood, girls and women with undiagnosed autism may present with burnout, depression, or chronic feelings of isolation because their difficulties were interpreted as personality traits rather than neurodevelopmental differences [5].

Reducing disparities among ASD patients requires action at multiple levels, including screening tools and clinical training that reflect a broader range of autistic presentations, especially in girls and culturally diverse populations [8].
